I've thought an awful lot about Nethack scoring for some time, but
every time I was about to bring up my ideas, I always backed out before
clicking the fatal button.  Now there seems to be a running discussion
I figured I'd post some of what I've been thinking.  I don't have
a full scoring system ready for review (at least, not one I'm
comfortable with showing to everyone), but maybe you guys'll find
this helpful.

(Apologies if this is duplicated, Google Groups gave me an error when I
made my first attempt at sending it.)


** Why should score go up for killing monsters? **

I'd say it's because that, contrary to the general current of
opinion here in the newsgroup, the primary goal in Nethack is not
necessarily to achieve ascension.

There are plenty of reasons to play Nethack.  Killing monsters is one
of them, and indeed probably more people play Nethack just to explore
and slay things than to ascend.  Many players have no idea whatsoever
that such a thing called ascension exists, yet they still enjoy the
game.  This newsgroup is filled with players who are steeped in the
lore of the game, who have read many of the spoilers and maybe even the
source code, and it is easy to forget this. Casual and unspoiled
players should not be forgotten in the scoring system.

Ascension is actually a fairly arbitrary event, as illustrated by the
sheer number of conduct ascensions.  You can play very atypically and
still ascend, and you can play very well, tactically speaking, and
never win. The way I see it, ascending, in Nethack, is a series of
arbitrary acts that become possible because the player has mastered the
"real" game enough in order to get to the places where they can be
accomplished.

Winning the game, of course, is a feat deserving of a score award.  But
I think it's a mistake to rate only those things that advance the
player towards ascension.  At best this is a very small number of
things: Nethack doesn't force you to do a great deal regarding
ascension, just collecting the major items, getting to the vibrating
square, getting the Amulet, then offering it on the appropriate High
Altar.  Many people never make it even to the Quest, but they
shouldn't receive a score of zero for doing so.  At worst, scoring
based only on progress made towards ascension would count all
ascensions equally.

There are many, many people who play Nethack without any hope of ever
ascending.  It seems out of reach to them.  Until you reach the point,
in playing a Roguelike, when winning seems like a plausible goal, then
going for a high score is basically the way to play.  Nethack's
current system works because if, upon dying, you think to yourself,
"Man, that was my best game yet," then very likely when you get to the
scoreboard it turns out that you have reached first place.  That is,
the score tends to match player expectations up through the mid-game.
I think this attribute should be kept at all costs, though fixed so it
doesn't become too exploitable in the late game.


** How do we ensure that any additional, or alternate, means of
awarding points don't fall victim to the current scoring problems? **

By limiting them in these ways:
1. If the award is for an act that gets easier over time, like earning
experience points, or can be performed indefinitely with no reduction
in difficulty, then the award should go down with each repetition.  So,
instead of scoring based on real experience, maybe it should be awarded
based on the square root of real experience?  Though this would make
the repeatedly-killing-Riders loophole less of a problem, it should
still probably be fixed.

2. If the award is based on something that can only be done a limited
number of times, then we can award linearly.  An example would be
awarding a set number of points according to the number of types of
monsters killed, with uniques counting as their own types.  An award
for intrinsics possessed would also work, though for very good games
this would tend to even out to about the same.

The award for artifacts owned at the end of the game is like this,
since there can only be one of each.

3. The inventory system does not impose a hard limit on the number of
things a player can carry, but it does provide a limit.  Thus, scoring
based on player possessions can be done linearly.  It may be possible
to acquire loot arbitrarily, but carrying it around is difficult.  If
we made it so that the player cannot acquire substantial loot on the
Astral Plane (maybe disabling polypiling there), then the player
wouldn't be able to just go to the high altar, cook himself up a
bunch of zorkmids, then ascend while overtaxed.

Zorkmids scored at one point each sounds about right, since the player
has to carry that money.  Collecting loot is a traditional source of
scoring for Roguelikes, and is arguably undervalued by the current
system: if you win with a dilithium crystal that's 4,500 bonus
points, but that's pocket-change compared with the millions you've
already got by this time. The problem with this is using nested bags of
holding (which does not always explode the bag, to my knowledge) to
increase capacity, so I'd suggest that the scoring algorithm not
count bags of holding so nested.

But gemstones have the potential to be worth huge numbers of points if
valued at their gold cost, due to their low weight, and polypiling
makes it easy to acquire lots of them under the current system, so to
prevent them from being the primary source of points, we'd either
have to do something like limit the score each type of gem could
provide, or make them more difficult to get from polymorphing items.
I'd consider the latter option, since gemstones aren't useful for
much right now except for being valuable.  Monster drops are another
potential source for gemstones, but the extinction code should
eventually cut down on the number of high-gem producing monsters in
very long games.


** Conduct ascensions **

I'm unsure conduct needs to be scored.  Maybe a modest award for each
conduct, but nothing more than this.  Many conducts revolve around
ideas that, when viewed outside the context of the conducts, require
foolish play.  I think that perhaps it might be cool to commemorate
conduct ascensions with special notification on the scoreboard, but not
necessarily to reward them greatly beyond the ascension bonus itself.
- John H.


